Reflection - Student Registration Page

1. What is the difference between radio buttons and checkboxes?
Radio buttons let the user pick only ONE option from a group (they share the
same "name" attribute, e.g. Gender). Checkboxes let the user pick zero, one
or many options independently (e.g. Hobbies).

2. What is the purpose of the <label> tag?
The <label> tag describes what an input field is for. It is linked to an input
using the "for" attribute matching the input's "id". Clicking the label focuses
or selects the input, and it helps screen readers, so it improves usability
and accessibility.

3. What is the difference between <th> and <td>?
<th> is a table header cell. It is bold and centered by default and tells the
browser and screen readers that the cell is a heading. <td> is a normal table
data cell that holds the actual content.

4. Why do we use semantic HTML elements?
Semantic elements like <header>, <nav>, <main>, <section> and <footer> describe
the meaning of the content, not just how it looks. They make code easier to
read and maintain, help screen readers navigate, and help search engines
understand the page structure.

